    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I sometimes get carried away with the, uh, \"mythical\" aspects of gardening. The yellowing leaves are indeed a sign of nitrogen deficiency, and you're wise to consider your options.\n\nAs for the blood meal, a half-acre plot is a significant area, and you'll want to apply it judiciously. A general rule of thumb is to apply 1-2 pounds of blood meal per 1,000 square feet, depending on the soil type and the specific needs of your kale crop.\n\nFor a half-acre plot, you're looking at around 10,000-20,000 square feet. So, for a conservative estimate, you could start with 1-2 pounds of blood meal, spread evenly across the plot. This should provide a boost of nitrogen without overloading the soil.\n\nHowever, keep in mind that blood meal is a concentrated source of nitrogen, and it's easy to overdo it. A good rule of thumb is to apply it in the early spring or fall, when the plants are actively growing, and then follow up with a balanced fertilizer later in the season.\n\nAnd, if I may say so, a little bit of caution is always a good thing when working with fertilizers, lest",
